```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": [
    "Original request, S1–S2",
    "Exercise instructions and environment context"
  ],
  "headline": "Intake mapped the sales-summary tool and tests to outcomes, boundaries, and recorded defaults.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "A person using sales records runs a Python command-line tool with CSV file paths."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Running the command on valid sales records prints the correct total revenue, revenue per region, and units per product."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "In scope: reading sales CSV files and printing the three requested summaries; S2 also requires tests. Out of scope: importing data into another system, a graphical interface, deployment, and additional reports."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The exercise says the repository is empty, so no existing repository behavior is identified for preservation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume `python sales_summary.py FILE [FILE ...]`; each UTF-8 CSV has a header with `region`, `product`, `unit_price`, and `quantity`. Print labeled total revenue, revenue grouped by region, and units grouped by product to stdout. Assume monetary values are decimal numbers and quantities are integers; output formatting remains a discovery question."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume exit code 0 on success and 1 for missing arguments, unreadable files, malformed CSV, missing columns, or invalid values; report a useful error on stderr and print no partial summary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume revenue is unit_price multiplied by quantity, summed across all supplied files; aggregate exact, trimmed region and product names, and use decimal arithmetic. Assume nonnegative prices and quantities and one shared, unspecified currency; these defaults exclude returns represented by negative values and currency conversion."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume missing required fields, blank region or product, non-finite or negative prices, and non-integer or negative quantities are invalid. Discovery should check for evidence of other sales-record conventions."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume each invocation owns in-memory aggregation state for its duration; source CSV files remain owned by their existing owners and are not modified. No persistent application state is required."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the invoking user can see stdout and stderr, subject to the surrounding environment's access controls. Separate invocations have independent state; no coordination or recovery from concurrent file changes is specified, and a restart rereads the files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Discovery should inspect the available Python runtime and record a compatible version; the request specifies Python but no version."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the Python standard library is sufficient; add no third-party dependency unless discovery finds it necessary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume the command needs file paths as arguments and read access to them, with no ports, environment variables, or configuration files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Separate argument and error handling, CSV parsing and validation, aggregation, and output formatting so each can be tested independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume standard-library unittest with `python -m unittest discover`."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Run the actual command against representative CSV files and check stdout and exit status for all three totals; also check stderr and nonzero exit status for invalid input. Run the test suite to confirm those checks pass."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Deliver a Python command-line tool usable from the resulting source checkout; execution needs a compatible Python runtime and read access to the supplied CSV files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No numerical performance, accessibility, security, or presentation bounds are specified. Assume a readable text summary and ordinary local file access, with no network access required."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The highest rejection risk is that actual CSV headers, revenue rules, returns, currency conventions, or expected output differ from the recorded defaults."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Inspect representative sales records or authoritative schema documentation early, then exercise the command and expected totals against representative examples."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 — Fact: deliver a Python CLI that reads sales CSVs and prints total revenue, revenue by region, and units by product; the named command-line target grants delivery authority for that source result, visible in the isolated checkout after final return and usable when invoked, with no deployed target requested. Assumptions: use the schema, arithmetic, validation, output and state defaults recorded above; each invocation is independent, holds aggregates only in memory, ends when the command exits, and exposes results through its process output under the environment's access controls. Discovery must first look for representative records, schema documentation, runtime details, and existing delivery conventions; it owns resolving the open schema, currency, returns, output-format, runtime, and concurrent-file-change questions, which block confidence in compatibility and the final implementation and boundary checks. If evidence is absent, the recorded defaults govern and rule out negative-value returns, currency conversion, and coordinated concurrent updates. No multi-person interactive product or shared session is requested, so actor identification, hidden shared state, session lifetime, and interactive runtime facilities do not apply. Discovery source hints: the repository or workspace, team and owners, relevant channels, internal sites and documents, and private repositories if available; none is verified by the request, and 'sales records' and 'revenue' are ambiguous terms. Keep any sensitive locator only in an authorized private store, never in KNOWLEDGE/. S2 — Fact: add tests for the requested behavior; the recorded unittest default applies unless discovery finds an established convention, and the tests become available in the checkout after final return and observable when run."
}
```
